Package: ringzero-desktop
Version: 0.1.0
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Architecture: amd64
Depends: ringzero-security (= 0.1.0), libwebkit2gtk-4.1-0, libgtk-3-0t64 | libgtk-3-0, libayatana-appindicator3-1, librsvg2-2
Recommends: pkexec | policykit-1
Maintainer: Ring Zero Security <abhijit@ringzerosecurity.com>
Homepage: https://ringzerosecurity.com
Vcs-Git: https://github.com/ringzerosec/jev-runtime-security
Vcs-Browser: https://github.com/ringzerosec/jev-runtime-security
Description: Ring Zero Security — desktop viewer
 A desktop window over the local Ring Zero daemon: agent sessions, the event
 timeline, detected threats, the review queue and daemon status.
 .
 The app holds a READ-ONLY API token, deliberately: an AI coding agent runs as
 the same user, and a full-scope token within that user's reach would let the
 agent turn enforcement off. Everything the window reads uses that token.
 .
 Changing enforcement does not. A write runs the equivalent `rz` command
 through pkexec, so polkit asks an administrator to authenticate and the
 command runs as root. An agent running as the developer cannot answer that
 prompt, which is the line being drawn. Without pkexec installed the app says
 so and names the `sudo rz ...` command to run instead.
 .
 Pulls in the GTK/WebKit stack, which is why it is a separate package: a
 headless server installs ringzero-security alone and stays free of it.
